#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231297 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "459aac20-b2e4-5d77-b131-717c9745d43c" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RCL Circuit"}] :question/id #uuid "20c07bb4-e264-5674-a7f0-9a833f68d037" :question/math-academy-id "e-21894" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fae88c8da0b50da2bc025cbac0dbc376be3b67b69b59823746873c6e7d102dcc.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The circuit is a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\cos(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $30\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.002\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 30I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C=\\dfrac{q}{C}=\\dfrac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n6\\cos(4t)=30I+500q+5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(30I+500q+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =30\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+500\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+100I=-\\dfrac{24}{5}\\sin(4t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231298 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231408 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6faa97d-4d8e-5d67-bb99-8aae6de5a840" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RCL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/id #uuid "6f2eeea4-7339-5f96-b3e0-fa160a791034" :question/math-academy-id "e-21895" :question/problem "![](images/08/085fe02b43301629af58379289fc9d1c49c90e71a83e0dd33f491ecf4b21bfc8.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\dfrac25\\,\\textrm{C}$ and the initial current is $\\dfrac34\\,\\textrm{A}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The circuit is a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 10I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C=\\dfrac{q}{C}=\\dfrac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n9=10I+4q+4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(9) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(10I+4q+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+\\dfrac52\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I=e^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I=e^{-2t}$ and $I=e^{-t/2}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=Ae^{-2t}+Be^{-t/2}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\dfrac25\\,\\textrm{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $\\dfrac34\\,\\textrm{A}.$\nNow, solving the Kirchhoff's law equation for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\dfrac94-\\dfrac52I-q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=\\dfrac34$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{9}{4}-\\frac{5}{2}I(0)-q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{40},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\dfrac34$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & =A{e}^{-2(0)}+B{e}^{-0/2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\nI'=-2Ae^{-2t}-\\dfrac12Be^{-t/2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting in $I'(0)=-\\dfrac1{40}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{40} & =-2A{e}^{-2(0)}-\\frac{1}{2}B{e}^{-0/2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{40} & =-2A-\\frac{1}{2}B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}=A+B \\\\ -\\frac{1}{40}=-2A-\\frac{1}{2}B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\dfrac{7}{30}$ and $B=\\dfrac{59}{60}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\dfrac{7}{30}e^{-2t}+\\dfrac{59}{60}e^{-t/2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231299 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231409 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2fbf2a2-d7d1-5b6e-9304-38d9ae1c60bb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RCL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/id #uuid "cc9247b8-2a3d-5559-af6a-0cf1d6fc7a96" :question/math-academy-id "e-21896" :question/problem "![](images/32/323324bc8d0ab017d30bc2a37e5e5e66784d64da6309fc131d56cb576ed2b003.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\cos(2t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $8\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=8\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 8I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{0.25} = 4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n6\\cos(2t)=8I+4q+4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(8I+4q+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+2\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+I=-3\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 2\\, \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t}+ I_c=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nI_c(t)=Ae^{-t}+Bte^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(2t)+\\beta\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+2(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ +(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-4\\alpha -3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -4\\alpha -3\\beta =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=\\dfrac{12}{25}$ and $\\beta=\\dfrac{9}{25}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nI_p(t)=\\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t)+\\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-t} + Bte^{-t} + \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = Ae^{-t} + Bte^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $I_p(t) = \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)\\approx I_p(t) = \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{12}{25}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{9}{25}\\right)^2} = \\dfrac35.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "459aac20-b2e4-5d77-b131-717c9745d43c" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RCL Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88cf3fcd-4ee7-54b3-8f65-afd877b34b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d^{2}I}{dt^{2}} + 2\\frac{dI}{dt} + 10I = \\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230329} {:answer/id #uuid "92a60b70-53e9-5c50-b827-3156624ac267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d^{2}I}{dt^{2}} + 4\\frac{dI}{dt} + 10I = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230330} {:answer/id #uuid "f807674a-f78c-5ad0-90d0-e7a9856a8afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d^{2}I}{dt^{2}} + 2\\frac{dI}{dt} + 10I = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230331} {:answer/id #uuid "49705029-53d3-529c-b9d6-898d96972adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d^{2}I}{dt^{2}} + 4\\frac{dI}{dt} + 10I = \\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230332} {:answer/id #uuid "771865f5-44c3-5631-b5be-4f5978c1c607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d^{2}I}{dt^{2}} + 10\\frac{dI}{dt} + 50I = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230333}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b3337933-3172-5525-8f80-11c7c62b3691" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230328} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eddd9073-1ad4-5279-b55d-a473698a2502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372824} {:answer/id #uuid "6c605f7c-79a8-5f06-9dbe-4f77a9c6e01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372825} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc010de-07f2-58cf-b74e-663d35181ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372826} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2f6f83-b0a3-5e59-9b35-1c3310efc000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+50I=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372827} {:answer/id #uuid "1887a856-4d06-5a2d-b254-5b287ec524da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1887a856-4d06-5a2d-b254-5b287ec524da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372828} :answer-field/id #uuid "9596a372-debc-57f5-9e3c-81666bd27a19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c2c7cc0-6b5e-525e-9e79-08e37b20c7d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-343955" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $40\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.005\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) in the circuit at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/16/169844e6d45393119ac1579cc89e34850401810bfcc1c7537d5b72851a0b2b02.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=40\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=40I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=20\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n12=40I+200q+20\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(12) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(40I+200q+20\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =40\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+200\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231408 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6faa97d-4d8e-5d67-bb99-8aae6de5a840" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RCL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab332032-59a9-576b-ad87-718419f4614e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t) =-\\frac{24}{5}e^{-3t} + \\frac{33}{5}e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230365} {:answer/id #uuid "c25d4d30-e667-5a62-9bf4-a8f0d09357b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t) = \\frac{15}{2}e^{-3t} - \\frac{33}{5}e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230366} {:answer/id #uuid "edc2eac1-4897-5627-bbd4-41d345581ff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t) = \\frac{33}{5}e^{-3t} + \\frac{24}{5}e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230367} {:answer/id #uuid "2a239042-4969-5069-908a-aee7da42c369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t) =-\\frac{33}{5}e^{-3t} + \\frac{15}{2}e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230368} {:answer/id #uuid "4eef4f40-7130-5116-91b8-dfbace36f4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t) = \\frac{15}{2}e^{-3t} + \\frac{33}{5}e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230369}] :answer-field/id #uuid "15657226-07d5-5f9f-9fc7-2af1559fad9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230364} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d19eb6a-404e-54bc-b559-30dea0cde985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t)=-\\frac{33}{5}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{15}{2}{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372836} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b99eeb-efb4-5ea9-a273-a8358cc30301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{15}{2}{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{33}{5}{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372837} {:answer/id #uuid "545cf06e-62be-5106-80f2-df116f2b8f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{15}{2}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{33}{5}{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372838} {:answer/id #uuid "699fb09b-da0b-5086-b8b5-517f256dd776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t)=-\\frac{24}{5}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{33}{5}{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372839} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b44c36-697c-54d6-b717-f23c70fba3be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{33}{5}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{24}{5}{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d19eb6a-404e-54bc-b559-30dea0cde985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t)=-\\frac{33}{5}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{15}{2}{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372836} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ff99686-d363-57d9-bcb7-55a023758c36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac407737-c497-5620-a022-43410b01e805" :question/math-academy-id "q-344036" :question/problem "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, and current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RCL circuit are related by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I+6q=12.\n$$\n\nFind an equation for the current in the circuit, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{9}{20}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $\\frac{9}{10}\\,\\text{A}.$" :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I+6q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(12) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-3t}$ and $I={e}^{-2t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-3t}+B{e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{9}{20}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $\\frac{9}{10}\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=12-5I-6q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=\\frac{9}{10}$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =12-5I(0)-6q(0) \\\\  & =12-5\\cdot \\frac{9}{10}-6\\cdot \\frac{9}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\frac{9}{10}$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10} & =A{e}^{-3(0)}+B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ \\frac{9}{10} & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3t}-2B{e}^{-2t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=\\frac{24}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{24}{5} & =-3A{e}^{-3(0)}-2B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ \\frac{24}{5} & =-3A-2B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10}=A+B \\\\ \\frac{24}{5}=-3A-2B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\frac{33}{5}$ and $B=\\frac{15}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\frac{33}{5}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{15}{2}{e}^{-2t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231409 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2fbf2a2-d7d1-5b6e-9304-38d9ae1c60bb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RCL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1bb9f4b-6c2e-5d6b-87d0-78e4c9a5b1f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230377} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d3d421-418f-598d-b619-f761de70afeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230378} {:answer/id #uuid "353a8f78-ce46-5d98-86a4-c93a4fd5aa63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230379} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9ff9c8-f28b-5bbc-b56a-9dca8a667374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230380} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8c51ea-ffd9-5c44-a039-8d1f95f96d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230381}] :answer-field/id #uuid "00eff77d-799b-55ba-877d-1720b58cb296" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230376}] :question/id #uuid "f4b2630f-dea5-5b20-90c5-3365567316a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-344172" :question/problem "![](images/0d/0d8f6711f76fa9e55f11087c99d0e626ebc665e0f44beeefe3f67e9d35159618.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "459aac20-b2e4-5d77-b131-717c9745d43c" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RCL Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf500311-ba0c-5251-a16d-ae6ce700c2e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+100I=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372913} {:answer/id #uuid "198292fb-0c70-5ea9-acb2-c7ed4d73d67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+200I=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372914} {:answer/id #uuid "17ba4a0e-ed7f-54bf-adfb-a5cffd850e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+100=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372915} {:answer/id #uuid "5b203598-dfae-5eba-a462-ef03871d8a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+100I=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372916} {:answer/id #uuid "de2d56c9-274b-5ce4-a18e-6325e1ac2464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+200I=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf500311-ba0c-5251-a16d-ae6ce700c2e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+100I=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372913} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b5f9ae5-8d9d-5c4b-9f0c-42d42f19c0c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9d96ca73-b13f-58db-8ae6-317919c23853" :question/math-academy-id "q-345149" :question/problem "![](images/48/4852aaf3945331346a68fe3f967658665f71d81be0e28fd0d34fd965c7045596.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.005\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)=10I+200q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(10I+200q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+200\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+100I=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231409 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2fbf2a2-d7d1-5b6e-9304-38d9ae1c60bb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RCL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b987082-a17c-53bc-bbed-1787c3c1d9f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372962} {:answer/id #uuid "43efd276-fd28-518a-9979-3e368fa83dd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372963} {:answer/id #uuid "60480ab5-1701-50a6-a4b1-145226b4cb76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372964} {:answer/id #uuid "19932d86-ad56-5844-9fee-75926100c043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372965} {:answer/id #uuid "15c98267-2c10-515e-b941-3e73d588b194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19932d86-ad56-5844-9fee-75926100c043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372965} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a9f2ba6-6d39-5c9f-8012-929dccaa68cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00e5ae50-dbfe-5fa5-ba4a-5369d82d33ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-345398" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b612821f53f661975a8db889792b44a08aff2e5ba6d6cbc3ccb2992ee048b0b.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.125\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.125}=8q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=6I+8q+\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(6I+8q+\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 100\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+8\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=100\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+6(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ +8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =100\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ (4\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-12\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =100\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha +12\\beta =100\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -12\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{5}{2}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{15}{2}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-4t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{15}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231409 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2fbf2a2-d7d1-5b6e-9304-38d9ae1c60bb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RCL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e6318b4-4cd9-5701-b150-2a0733b155ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372969} {:answer/id #uuid "1a8b6266-7ed5-5a6b-9b66-7b50fddce8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{289}}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372970} {:answer/id #uuid "eec97a92-b4ef-5391-adae-1c20fde5ca29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{289}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372971} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2a1495-7de8-5966-a4b1-93ec356eaf40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372972} {:answer/id #uuid "90aee7b4-f854-5fa5-bf96-3cf3db76695e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90aee7b4-f854-5fa5-bf96-3cf3db76695e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372973} :answer-field/id #uuid "392d36d7-2200-5901-9575-08503600f500" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7973b7d7-65db-5e2d-96ac-3fbab2d1e49a" :question/math-academy-id "q-345400" :question/problem "![](images/28/28bb1b428a1c187234f0baae580c0fd83eba234c869225f78132f8d110d7a2bc.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $4\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.5\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=4\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=4I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.5}=2q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)=4I+2q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4I+2q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+I=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-16\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-16\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))+2(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\ +(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-15\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+(-8\\alpha -15\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-15\\alpha +8\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\ -8\\alpha -15\\beta =-6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{48}{289}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{90}{289}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{48}{289}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{90}{289}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}+\\frac{48}{289}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{90}{289}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{48}{289}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{90}{289}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{48}{289}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{90}{289}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{48}{289})}^{2}+{(\\frac{90}{289})}^{2}}=\\frac{6}{17}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231409 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2fbf2a2-d7d1-5b6e-9304-38d9ae1c60bb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RCL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bc2a4da-03ce-57e3-9b70-0befb4d403ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385936} {:answer/id #uuid "b28b624a-4185-5a97-a3c0-80852dfb8b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385937} {:answer/id #uuid "0071c68e-e3f7-5dd2-894d-a48eb8c00d96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385938} {:answer/id #uuid "536a5b8c-39ac-51e5-b1e1-07760402e9b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385939} {:answer/id #uuid "fe4b402b-cde9-50f4-b8b9-aca9ab3269d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b28b624a-4185-5a97-a3c0-80852dfb8b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385937} :answer-field/id #uuid "451c975f-1f1e-5645-aa6b-851c1bdb0a4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd487be7-9e9e-5c43-b82c-f04901db73d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-345412" :question/problem "![](images/70/70072e2a4599c8b1b43ff1782ee270076a384657ab02c7aeec1a3ae87c7674fc.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $4\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=4\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=4I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=4I+4q+\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4I+4q+\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+4{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+Bt{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ +4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-12\\alpha -5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +12\\beta =39\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -12\\alpha -5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{15}{13}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{36}{13}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{15}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{36}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+Bt{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{15}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{36}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+Bt{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{15}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{36}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{15}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{36}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{15}{13})}^{2}+{(\\frac{36}{13})}^{2}}=3.\n$$"}]}
